{"body": "**A conditional target-indexed aggregation account**\n\nI will answer the P7/P9 question with a construction that is mine, not a rewriting of C0. It does not adopt WHL or RSS as the grammar of the answer, and it does not install any of the repairs proposed in the unendorsed E009 criticism.\n\nThe construction separates three things that P9 tends to run together:\n\n1. **Local possibilities** — for each booking, the set of reservations left unresolved by the records.\n2. **An aggregation rule** — how local possibilities are combined into a global availability claim.\n3. **A compatibility relation** — which combinations of local possibilities are jointly feasible. P9 says the snapshot does not name physical resources, so this relation is not supplied. It only raises the conditional possibility that booking A’s high alternative and booking B’s high alternative might be incompatible if they share an unnamed resource.\n\nP9 already says that intervals lose correlations. My addition is a target distinction: the compatibility objection is not primarily a criticism of a record, a booking history, or the capacity arithmetic. It is a criticism of an aggregation rule that treats the global possibilities as the independent product of per-booking possibilities. That product assumption is not stated in P9. P9 only says that the interval may lose correlations and that the incompatibility is invisible in the snapshot. So the missing item is a relation on combinations, not a local reservation value.\n\n**Does P9 require a change to global availability?**\n\nConditionally, yes or no.\n\nIf an account presents the min/max interval only as a **bound**, then the compatibility objection does not require changing the bound. The interval can remain correct while being loose. If an account presents the interval as the **set of possible global availabilities**, as a convex summary, or as sufficient to answer “how much is available?”, then the objection requires a change. The change can be to parameterise the account by a compatibility relation, or to weaken the claim: the interval is a projection under an explicit independence assumption.\n\nIt does not require changing local per-booking alternatives, the supersession rule, or the capacity subtraction. It requires changing the global aggregation rule’s claim to completeness.\n\n**What is being questioned in P7’s sense?**\n\nP7 says one can criticise a reconstruction rule, a particular record, a booking’s history, the snapshot as a whole, or an assumption such as that logical time is meaningful. It also says a criticism of the rule “higher revision always supersedes” is not the same as a criticism of a record that carries a high revision but an implausible quantity.\n\nThe P9 compatibility objection is closest to a criticism of a **reconstruction rule at the global aggregation level**, and, if the rule is understood to depend on an unstated assumption that no unnamed shared resource constrains combinations, a criticism of that **source assumption**. It is not a criticism of a particular record, a booking’s history, or the meaningfulness of logical time. It can be a criticism of the snapshot as a whole only in the sense of **under-specification**, not in the sense of inconsistency.\n\nThis distinguishes it from P7’s examples of “this snapshot is inconsistent”: two active holds for one booking at the same greatest revision; total reservations exceeding capacity; logical time behind an expiry. The compatibility objection can hold even when total reservations do not exceed capacity and no local conflict exists. It is a different kind of target: not data contradicting data, but a summary missing a constraint on combinations.\n\n**Added distinctions**\n\nFirst, **inconsistency versus incompleteness**. P7’s examples are inconsistency claims about the snapshot or its records. P9’s compatibility objection is a completeness or correlation claim about a summary. It says some combinations inside the interval may be impossible, not that the interval’s bounds are false.\n\nSecond, **independence of tie-breaking versus independence of feasibility**. C0 says different bookings need not share the same choice; no global tiebreak is forced. That does not entail that their choices are jointly feasible. P9’s objection is about feasibility correlations, not about imposing a shared tiebreak.\n\nThird, **rule target versus data target**. The unendorsed E009 criticism notes that WHL’s `Conjecture` is `Snapshot → Prop` and cannot distinguish “the rule is bad” from “this record is corrupt” from “the assumption is false.” I do not adopt WHL, but the underlying distinction is useful: the compatibility objection is a rule or assumption target, not a data target, unless a resource model is added to the data. One can reject the aggregation rule while accepting every record and the supersession rule. One can also accept the aggregation rule while rejecting the resource-model claim as speculative.\n\nFourth, **bounds versus possible set**. A min/max interval can be a valid bound while the set of possible availabilities is non-convex or has holes. The compatibility objection attacks the set or completeness claim, not necessarily the bound.\n\n**A discriminating case, from my construction**\n\nCapacity 100. Booking A has alternatives {0, 10}; booking B has alternatives {0, 10}. A shared resource R has capacity 10, and each high alternative consumes 10 of R. The snapshot does not name R.\n\nCapacity-only independent aggregation gives possible sums {0, 10, 20}, hence availabilities {100, 90, 80} and interval [80, 100]. Under R, (10, 10) is infeasible, so sums are {0, 10} and availabilities are {100, 90}. The interval [80, 100] still bounds the feasible availabilities, but 80 is not achievable.\n\nIf an account says “availability can be 80,” it is false under R. If it says “availability is between 80 and 100,” it is true but loose. The criticism targets the claim that 80 is achievable — that is, the independent-product aggregation — not the capacity, not the local alternatives, not the records. This example is my addition. C0 P9 gives no numbers or named resource.\n\n**What additional observation would matter**\n\nA named resource model mapping booking alternatives to resource consumption; an operational trace where both high alternatives are accepted but one fails despite capacity; an explicit policy that capacity is the only global constraint; or an explicit statement that global availability is only a projection under independence. Without one of these, P9’s compatibility objection remains a conditional possibility, not an observed inconsistency. That is the obstruction: the frozen material does not contain the compatibility relation, and ordinary prose can mark the gap but cannot decide whether the gap is real.\n\n**Relation to E009**\n\nThe selected E009 occurrence is unendorsed and is not additional warehouse evidence. Its section on non-convexity and cross-booking correlations already records that WHL’s independent min/max bounds may include an impossible combination and proposes a resource model or possible-worlds set, not installed. Its section on criticism targets and source assumptions records that WHL’s `Conjecture`/`Refutation` cannot distinguish rule, record, and assumption targets, and proposes `Account`, `Target`, and `Assumption`, not installed.\n\nI do not install or endorse those repairs. I use the collision between those two loci to formulate the target-indexed aggregation account: the compatibility objection is not merely a missing primitive; it is a different kind of target from P7’s data-inconsistency examples. Whether WHL or RSS can express it is a separate question.\n\n**What remains unresolved**\n\nC0 does not say whether any shared physical resource exists. P9 says the incompatibility is invisible. The P9 criticism that exposing alternatives without a policy pushes the problem downstream remains disputed; my account does not resolve it. It only says that if one exposes alternatives, the compatibility relation is part of what must be exposed or explicitly assumed. The P7 target taxonomy may need an added subcategory, “global aggregation target.” Adding it is my proposal, not C0.\n\nThe next discriminating question is: for a given global availability account, does it claim the min/max interval is (a) merely a bound, (b) the complete set of possible availabilities, or (c) a projection under a stated independence assumption? If (b), and no compatibility relation is supplied, then P9’s objection requires weakening or parameterising the account. If (a) or (c), it requires at most a target label. What would decide is a named shared resource or an operational counterexample.", "commitments": "**A conditional target-indexed aggregation account**\n\nI will answer the P7/P9 question with a construction that is mine, not a rewriting of C0. It does not adopt WHL or RSS as the grammar of the answer, and it does not install any of the repairs proposed in the unendorsed E009 criticism.\n\nThe construction separates three things that P9 tends to run together:\n\n1. **Local possibilities** — for each booking, the set of reservations left unresolved by the records.\n2. **An aggregation rule** — how local possibilities are combined into a global availability claim.\n3. **A compatibility relation** — which combinations of local possibilities are jointly feasible. P9 says the snapshot does not name physical resources, so this relation is not supplied. It only raises the conditional possibility that booking A’s high alternative and booking B’s high alternative might be incompatible if they share an unnamed resource.\n\nP9 already says that intervals lose correlations. My addition is a target distinction: the compatibility objection is not primarily a criticism of a record, a booking history, or the capacity arithmetic. It is a criticism of an aggregation rule that treats the global possibilities as the indepen